WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:08.320
Sometimes a new exciting opportunity presents
itself, when we are completely happy about

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:10.240
our existing life situation.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.080
If that happens, we are forced to think about
opportunity costs.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.320
This is because when we&nbsp;
look at the new opportunity,&nbsp;

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.720
we compare it with our current situation and

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.840
think about what is at stake if we would go
for it.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.200
This often happens without us being able to
see the full picture.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:34.560
Economists define opportunity cost as the
benefit we forego, as we trade-off one alternative

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:35.200
to keep another.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.120
— hoping we make the best choice.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.840
Although they are often less obvious and sometimes
speculative, opportunity costs can be huge,

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.800
therefore, learning about them makes sense.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.080
Imagine that we are trying to calculate the
true cost of going to college.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:56.800
One factor can be tuition, which in our case
is 10,000 dollars.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.440
Since that’s our only cost, you might conclude
that the cost of college is 10,000 dollars

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:02.080
a year.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:04.960
But to economists, that's not quite right.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.440
Because we have to add the money we could
have earned by working, instead of attending

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:09.920
college.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:16.960
Let’s say that by working full-time in a
local restaurant, you can earn $20,000 a year

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:17.520
.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:24.960
The true costs of going to college are, therefore,
$10,000 tuition plus $20,000 in opportunity

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:25.920
costs.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.320
By studying for a degree, we trade off 30,000
dollars a year.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.240
Although, that's only half the story because
it also goes the other way around.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:41.520
Assuming we go to college for four years,
we trade-off $120,000 for an education that

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.200
leaves us with empty pockets, many ideas and
a degree.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:51.680
However, once we are better educated, we might
be able to make $40,000 as managers instead

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:54.160
of the $20,000 in service.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.160
In such a case, not going to college ends
up being much more expensive.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.600
Do you understand the concept?

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:02.080
Show us!

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:07.280
Calculate the opportunity costs of a high
school student who drops the idea of earning

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:11.120
a degree in medicine, and instead chooses
to help in the family business.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.960
The cost for tuition and living is $2,000
a month.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:20.000
It takes a total of 6 years to finish university.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.040
After obtaining her degree she works in the
field of medicine for 34 years earning $10,000

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:28.640
a month and spending $3,000 on monthly living
expenses.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:33.840
If she is entering the family business she
earns $3,500 a month right away for the next

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.840
40 years with zero living expenses.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.800
What’s financially the better choice?

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.320
Post your calculations in the

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:49.840
comments below!

